/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Workflows_ReleasePackages Release Packages
 * @ingroup Foundation_Workflows_Delivery
 * @brief Build validated archives and publish immutable GitHub Release assets.
 *
 * `.github/workflows/release.yml` converts an existing version tag into the
 * downloadable precompiled packages consumed through `find_package`. This is
 * separate from the @ref Foundation_Workflows_ContinuousIntegration workflow.
 * Native CI proves every main-branch change, while the release workflow
 * rebuilds and publishes the exact tagged source.
 *
 * @section FoundationReleasePackagesTriggers Triggers and Publication
 *
 * Pushing a tag beginning with `v` starts the workflow and publishes the
 * resulting GitHub Release automatically. The tag must match the version in
 * `CMakeLists.txt`. Stable and release-candidate examples are:
 *
 * @code
 * v1.0.0
 * v1.0.0-rc.1
 * @endcode
 *
 * `workflow_dispatch` requires an existing tag. By default it only creates
 * temporary workflow artifacts for inspection. Selecting its `publish` input
 * additionally creates the GitHub Release. This makes manual packaging safe to
 * rehearse without publishing a release accidentally.
 *
 * @section FoundationReleasePackagesMatrix Package Matrix
 *
 * | Package identity | Runner | Archive |
 * | ---------------- | ------ | ------- |
 * | `macos_arm64` | `macos-15` | `.tar.gz` |
 * | `linux_gcc_x64` | `ubuntu-24.04` | `.tar.gz` |
 * | `linux_clang_x64` | `ubuntu-24.04` | `.tar.gz` |
 * | `windows_msvc_x64` | `windows-2025-vs2026` | `.zip` |
 * | `windows_clang_x64` | `windows-2025-vs2026` | `.zip` |
 *
 * Every matrix job performs a fresh Release export and executes the standalone
 * package consumer before creating its archive. Packages do not include Debug
 * libraries or examples. Every package includes `LICENSE`, `README.md`, and
 * `CHANGELOG.md`. Each archive contains one top-level directory named
 * `Foundation-<release>-<preset>` and is accompanied by a `.sha256` file.
 *
 * A release-candidate asset uses the tag suffix in its filename even though
 * the CMake package reports the numeric project version. For example,
 * `v1.0.0-rc.1` produces
 * `Foundation-1.0.0-rc.1-macos_arm64.tar.gz`.
 *
 * @section FoundationReleasePackagesConsumption Consuming a Download
 *
 * Extract the archive, then add its top-level directory to CMake's prefix
 * search path:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * cmake -S . -B build \
 *   -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/path/to/Foundation-1.0.0-macos_arm64
 * @endcode
 *
 * The consuming project uses only the exported CMake target:
 *
 * @code{cmake}
 * find_package(Foundation 1.0 CONFIG REQUIRED)
 * target_link_libraries(MyApplication PRIVATE Foundation::Foundation)
 * @endcode
 *
 * `Foundation_DIR` may instead point directly to
 * `<prefix>/lib/cmake/Foundation`. Consumers must select the package matching
 * their operating system, architecture, compiler ABI, and Release policy.
 * GitHub Actions artifacts are short-lived validation output; GitHub Release
 * assets are the supported downloadable distribution channel.
 *
 * @section FoundationReleasePackagesReleaseProcedure Release Procedure
 *
 * Before creating a publishing tag:
 *
 * 1. complete the applicable gates in @ref Foundation_Workflows_ReleaseV1;
 * 2. ensure native CI is green on the exact commit;
 * 3. create and push an annotated tag;
 * 4. wait for all package jobs and checksum verification to succeed;
 * 5. inspect the published release and test a clean external download.
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * git tag -a v1.0.0-rc.1 -m "Foundation v1.0.0-rc.1"
 * git push origin v1.0.0-rc.1
 * @endcode
 *
 * A failed package job prevents publication. A tag is a release decision, not
 * merely a request to test an arbitrary branch, and should not be moved after
 * packages have been published.
 */
